A recent study tracked over 116,000 teens and found heavy screen use linked to poor sleep.
Why does this matter? Because when kids sleep poorly, they eat worse and move less. It is all connected.
So how much screen time is too much? Theres no single answer. What matters is what screens replace: family time, outdoor play, sleep, meals together.
The Institute of Nutrition and Fitness Sciences (INFS) breaks down how screens affect your childs sleep, eating and energy and discusses what you can actually do.
How Much Screen Time Is Healthy For Kids? Age-By-Age Guide
Theres no magic screen time limit that works for everyone. The real question is its impact. Does screen time improve your childs life or crowd it out?
The American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) focuses on three things:
What your child watches (content matters)
Why theyre watching (educational vs. entertainment)
What happens when screens are off (does it crowd out healthy activities?)

Screen time becomes a problem when it regularly pushes out sleep, activity, meals, learning or family time.
Screen Time Recommendations By Age
The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends
Babies and toddlers (under 2): No screen time. Period.
Ages 2–4: Max one hour per day. Less is always better.

For ages 5–17, the Canadian 24-Hour Movement Guidelines use a benchmark of no more than 2 two hours of recreational screen time daily . This is alongside adequate sleep and at least 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous physical activity.
These arent strict rules, theyre starting points. Naturally, a 5-year-old needs more supervision than a 15-year-old.
Effects Of Screen Time On Child Development
Screens hurt more than eyesight and posture. Research shows heavy screen use changes how kids sleep, eat, move and feel.
